{#
  A speaker's name, rendered once and used in both places that need it.

  It is a macro rather than the same conditional written out twice for a
  reason beyond duplication: the environment runs with `trim_blocks=True`
  (see `sturnus.application.documents._build_environment`), which eats the
  newline that follows a block tag. A line ending in `{% endif %}` therefore
  loses its own line break, so a blank line written after it survives as a
  single newline -- and a single newline in Markdown is a *soft* break, which
  renders on the same line. That is how the speaker's name used to run
  straight into the words they said.

  Ending the line with `}}` instead of a block tag puts the whitespace back
  under the template author's control, so a blank line below means a blank
  line in the output.
-#}
{% macro speaker(s) -%}
{% if s.external_user_id -%}
@[{{ s.external_display_name | md }}](mention://user/{{ s.external_user_id }}) ([{{ s.discord_display_name | md }}](https://discord.com/users/{{ s.discord_user_id }}))
{%- else -%}
[{{ s.discord_display_name | md }}](https://discord.com/users/{{ s.discord_user_id }})
{%- endif %}
{%- endmacro %}
{% if channel %}**Channel:** [{{ channel.label | md }}]({{ channel.url }})

{% endif %}**Date:** @[{{ date_label }}](mention://date/{{ date_iso }}) · {{ started }}, {{ duration_minutes }} min · {{ participants | length }} participant{{ '' if participants | length == 1 else 's' }}

## Participants

{# One list item per line, with no blank line between them: a blank line
   would make this a "loose" list, which Markdown renders with a paragraph
   around every item and a gap between them. The transcript below wants the
   opposite -- there the blank line is what separates the attribution from
   the words. #}
{% for s in participants %}
- {{ speaker(s) }}
{% endfor %}

## Transcript

{% for block in blocks %}
**{{ block.time }}** · {{ speaker(block.speaker) }}

{{ block.text | md }}

{% endfor %}
